@*
    A read view of one markdown sample.

    Chrome, not a component: it frames an example and has no behaviour of its own.
    Every markdown story wants the same parse-and-render, and repeating it in each
    one is how a sample and its rendering drift apart.

    The source is not here any more. It lives in the story's own Code and source
    disclosure, beside the markup that produced the view, because the call and the
    text it was handed are one thing to read — so a page passes the same sample to
    `Story.Source` and to this. ParseDocument, not Parse: this chapter is about
    markdown, where a `##` is a heading.

    The knowledge parameters are forwarded verbatim rather than reinterpreted.
    A knowledge story wants the same rendering every other markdown story gets, and
    a frame that quietly rendered a `meta` fence differently from the MarkdownView
    it wraps would be documenting itself.
*@

<div class="sb-stack" data-testid="@TestId">
    <MarkdownView Blocks="Blocks"
                  ShowDiagramSource="@ShowDiagramSource"
                  RenderKnowledgeMetadata="@RenderKnowledgeMetadata"
                  KnowledgeFolder="@KnowledgeFolder"
                  OnKnowledgeNavigate="@OnKnowledgeNavigate"
                  KnowledgeHrefFor="@KnowledgeHrefFor" />
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>The markdown to render. The same string goes to the story's
    /// <c>Source</c>, which is where a reader reads it.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public string Source { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    [Parameter]
    public string? TestId { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether a diagram drawn from a fence keeps its own source
    /// disclosure. Off by default here, unlike in the library: a story shows the
    /// sample's markdown in its own Code and source column, so the disclosure
    /// under the picture would be the same fence a second time. The one story
    /// that is about the disclosure turns it back on.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool ShowDiagramSource { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Passed straight to <c>MarkdownView</c>. Off here for the same
    /// reason it is off there: most markdown is not a knowledge document.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool RenderKnowledgeMetadata { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Which knowledge folder the sample is pretending to come from.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public KnowledgeFolder KnowledgeFolder { get; set; } = KnowledgeFolder.Unknown;

    /// <summary>Raised with the reference a reader activated in a metadata block.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<KnowledgeReference> OnKnowledgeNavigate { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Resolves a metadata reference to a link target.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public Func<KnowledgeReference, string?>? KnowledgeHrefFor { get; set; }

    // Parsed when the source changes rather than on every render. These are
    // constants in practice, so this parses once.
    private IReadOnlyList<MdBlock> Blocks => _blocks ??= MarkdownPreview.ParseDocument(Source);

    private IReadOnlyList<MdBlock>? _blocks;

    protected override void OnParametersSet() => _blocks = null;
}
